WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.958
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.916 --> 00:00:07.670
ROGER MARTINEZ: If
you need to deploy

00:00:07.670 --> 00:00:10.670
a large number of
identical virtual machines,

00:00:10.670 --> 00:00:12.830
then Google Cloud's
Managed Instance Groups

00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:14.450
is the best way to do that.

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:16.280
But its autoscaling
feature is what

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:17.685
takes it to the next level.

00:00:17.685 --> 00:00:19.310
So let's take a look
at a configuration

00:00:19.310 --> 00:00:21.260
to see what it looks like.

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:23.740
So let's start by going over
here to the navigation menu

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:25.750
and going down to
Compute Engine and then

00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:28.285
over to Instance Groups.

00:00:28.285 --> 00:00:29.660
So what I have on
this project is

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:31.430
one managed instance
group called web-app.

00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:33.710
So let's click here to
go to the overview page,

00:00:33.710 --> 00:00:35.450
and we'll see that
this managed instance

00:00:35.450 --> 00:00:38.450
group has three instances only.

00:00:38.450 --> 00:00:40.640
So each instance in
this instance group

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:44.750
is just an HTTP application
that returns some basic instance

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:46.070
information up here.

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:48.380
It also has some
built-in functionality

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:51.140
that lets me increase
the load on each instance

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:54.090
in the form of CPU utilization.

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:58.420
So let's initiate that on
each of these machines.

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:04.209
Now on the second
one, and the third.

00:01:04.209 --> 00:01:06.890
So now, on each of
these instances,

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:11.020
the application is
consuming more and more CPU.

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:12.640
As you can see in
the Overview page,

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:15.710
I have autoscaling enabled on
this managed instance group.

00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:17.833
Let's take a look at
the configuration.

00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:23.760
So what we have is a
regional managed instance

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:28.160
group, distributed across three
zones in the US Central region.

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:30.920
And if you go down here,
you'll find the autoscaling

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:31.950
configuration.

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:33.740
If you have an existing
managed instance

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:35.870
group without
autoscaling enabled,

00:01:35.870 --> 00:01:38.060
you'll have instead
down here a button that

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:40.700
says Configure autoscaling.

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:42.740
So I'm going to talk
about a few of the options

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:46.040
that you have in the
autoscaling configuration.

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:48.710
First, you'll want to choose
what you want your autoscaling

00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:49.760
to be triggered by.

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:52.310
And you can choose from any
of these three metrics--

00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:56.720
CPU utilization, load-balancing
utilization, and Stackdriver

00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:59.630
monitoring metrics.

00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:01.130
Depending on your
use case, you can

00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:03.530
choose one of these
or a combination

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:05.210
by just clicking Add new metric.

00:02:13.930 --> 00:02:17.260
Next, you'll want to choose
a minimum and a maximum.

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:19.090
I'll set my minimum
to 3, because I

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:20.920
have a regional
managed instance group,

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:23.890
and I want to make sure I have
at least one instance in each

00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:26.310
of my three zones.

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:28.710
If you know that it takes
a while for your instances

00:02:28.710 --> 00:02:30.780
to initialize after
boot time, there

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:33.970
are three settings that you'll
want to pay attention to.

00:02:33.970 --> 00:02:37.180
So the first one is
the cooldown period.

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:40.050
So the cooldown period is
also known as the application

00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:41.970
initialization period.

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:44.760
It's important because when
an application on an instance

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:47.280
is initializing,
the usage might not

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:49.420
reflect normal circumstances.

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:52.020
So the autoscaler will
not take into account

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:54.120
the usage data from
this time period

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:56.940
when it decides to
scale in or out.

00:02:56.940 --> 00:03:00.150
Second is the scaling
controls down here.

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:01.890
So scaling controls
let you set a limit

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:05.130
to how quickly the autoscaler
scales your instance group down

00:03:05.130 --> 00:03:08.710
to avoid any sudden drops
in the number of instances.

00:03:08.710 --> 00:03:11.760
So this isn't required, but
it will help you optimize cost

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:15.210
by avoiding turning down too
many instances too quickly,

00:03:15.210 --> 00:03:17.970
only to have to wait for new
ones provisioned immediately

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:19.800
after.

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:22.800
And finally, there's
predictive autoscaling.

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:25.680
So predictive autoscaling
feeds your instance

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:28.170
groups' historical
trends and current load

00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:30.270
into a machine-learning
model so that you can

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:32.378
anticipate autoscale events.

00:03:32.378 --> 00:03:34.920
So this is going to improve the
availability, because instead

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:39.090
of scaling up reactively due
to a metric threshold like CPU,

00:03:39.090 --> 00:03:42.780
the instance group will scale
up in anticipation of the load

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:46.450
that it predicts will happen
based on real-world usage.

00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:48.540
So if your application
takes time to start up,

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:50.790
this is going to be
very valuable in helping

00:03:50.790 --> 00:03:53.220
ensure that your instance
group is ready to handle

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:55.200
an increasing load
when it happens,

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:58.680
without having to wait too
long for initialization.

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:01.740
Together, predictive
autoscaling, scaling controls,

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:03.600
and an accurate
cooldown period will

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:07.690
increase availability and
optimize spending on resources.

00:04:07.690 --> 00:04:12.630
So if we go back to
our Overview page,

00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:14.730
we'll see that the
autoscaler has already

00:04:14.730 --> 00:04:16.680
started to provision
additional instances

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:19.950
to accommodate the
CPU load of the group.

00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:22.410
Once that load is
reduced, it'll scale back

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:23.790
according to the
scaling controls

00:04:23.790 --> 00:04:25.790
that I specified before.

00:04:25.790 --> 00:04:28.910
That was a super quick look at
the autoscaling configuration

00:04:28.910 --> 00:04:30.502
of a managed instance group.

00:04:30.502 --> 00:04:32.210
When you're using
managed instance groups

00:04:32.210 --> 00:04:33.830
on Google Cloud for
your application,

00:04:33.830 --> 00:04:37.070
its autoscaling is a really
useful and powerful feature.

00:04:37.070 --> 00:04:39.110
With the right settings
and configuration,

00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:41.150
you can increase
your availability

00:04:41.150 --> 00:04:45.020
while reducing costs associated
with compute resources.

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:48.070
[MUSIC PLAYING]

